 ],
 "literal": "Bigger than the earth indeed, higher than the sky / deeper than the water indeed, on the mountain slopes / taking flowers of the black-stemmed Kurinji / rich honey is made, with the man of the land, my friendship!",
 "translation": "Bigger than the earth, higher than the sky, and far deeper than the waters is my love for the man of the mountain slopes, where bees build rich honeycombs from the blossoms of the black-stemmed Kurinji.",
 "modern": "நிலத்தை விடப் பெரியது; வானத்தை விட உயர்ந்தது; கடலை விட ஆழமானது; கருமையான கொம்புகளை உடைய குறிஞ்சிப் பூக்களைக் கொண்டு தேனீக்கள் பெரிய தேன்கூட்டைக் கட்டும் மலைநாட்டைச் சேர்ந்த தலைவனுடன் எனக்கு இருக்கும் நட்பு.",
 "explanation": "In this famous Kurinci poem, the heroine (talaivi) speaks to her close friend (toḻi) about the immense depth of her love for the hero. She compares her love to the vastness of the earth, the height of the sky, and the unfathomable depth of the ocean. The imagery of the mountain slopes where bees make rich honey from the black-stemmed Kurinji flowers represents the rare and precious nature of their union. Just as the Kurinji blooms once in many years to yield the finest honey, their love is a rare, slow-growing, and sweet treasure. The key words are 'nilattinum peritu' (bigger than the earth) and 'naṭpu' (friendship/love), which establish the cosmic scale of her affection.",
